# Copy to .env.local and fill in the values.
#
# ── SECURITY ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This file lists NAMES ONLY. Never put a real secret in a file that is
# committed. The real values must be set in the DEPLOYMENT ENVIRONMENT
# (Vercel → Project Settings → Environment Variables, for Production and
# Preview) and, for local development, in `.env.local` — which is gitignored.
#
# There are NO committed fallbacks for the secrets below. If a variable is
# missing the app fails loudly instead of using a key that is already in the
# repository:
#   * SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY missing → admin console shows "not configured",
#     every admin action returns an error, and Clerk sign-in cannot create a
#     marketplace account (see the note on the Clerk bridge below).
#   * NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY missing → the sign-in panel reports
#     that sign-in is not configured, instead of failing with a blank error.
#   * JOBS_SECRET missing              → /api/jobs/run answers 503.
#
# See README §1d for the full list and where each value comes from.

# ── Public (browser-exposed) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These are public by design: the anon key is what every browser gets, and RLS
# is what protects the data. `src/lib/supabase/config.ts` carries the same
# values as a literal fallback so the app builds on a host with no variables.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://ecejkevdfpzfvwfjjqwc.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=sb_publishable_nTmbEq4kh7viHqVFiSxtmw_m7Yh5-QI

# Public base URL of this app. Used for the Clerk OAuth redirect URLs
# (`/auth/sso-callback` and `/auth/continue`). Must match the origins allowed
# on the Clerk application, or the OAuth round trip is rejected with
# "redirect URL not allowed". See README §1c.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://naijamart-marketplace-opal.vercel.app
# Optional alias read by `src/lib/clerk/config.ts`, which prefers this name.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=

# ── Clerk (identity provider) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Clerk replaces Appwrite. It is the only auth provider: Google, Apple,
# LinkedIn and an emailed one-time code. There is no password anywhere.
#
# The publishable key is PUBLIC by design. It identifies the Clerk instance to
# the browser SDK; it authorises nothing. Get it from
# Clerk Dashboard → your application → API keys (`pk_test_…` / `pk_live_…`).
#
# NOTE: unlike the Supabase values, this one has NO literal fallback in code.
# A wrong fallback would silently point a fork at someone else's Clerk
# instance, so the app reports "sign-in is not configured" instead.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# Server-only Clerk secret key (`sk_test_…` / `sk_live_…`).
# NEVER prefix it with NEXT_PUBLIC_: that would inline it into the browser
# bundle and hand it to every visitor.
#
# The app does NOT read this value itself — `@clerk/nextjs` does, from
# `process.env.CLERK_SECRET_KEY`, on the server only. It is required for
# `auth()` / `currentUser()` to verify a session.
# ROTATE THIS if it has ever been pasted into a chat, a log or a screenshot.
CLERK_SECRET_KEY=

# The Clerk OAuth application's own redirect URLs are configured in the Clerk
# Dashboard (not here). For each provider set:
#   Allowed redirect URL:  <NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL>/auth/sso-callback
#   (and http://localhost:3000/auth/sso-callback for local development)
# In Clerk Dashboard → Configure → Paths, set:
#   Sign-in URL:    /auth
#   Sign-up URL:    /signup
#   After sign-out: /auth

# ── Server-only secrets — REQUIRED, no fallback ─────────────────────────────
# NEVER prefix any of these with NEXT_PUBLIC_: that inlines them into the
# browser bundle.

# Supabase service-role key. Bypasses RLS; the admin console and the push
# dispatcher use it, and so does the Clerk→Supabase bridge
# (`src/app/auth/clerk-actions.ts` → `src/lib/clerk/bridge.ts`), which has to
# look up and create marketplace users by email. Get it from
# Supabase → Project Settings → API Keys (the `service_role` secret, or a
# `sb_secret_…` key).
# ROTATE THIS if it has ever been committed, pasted into a chat, a log or a
# screenshot. Removing it from the repo does not make the old value dead.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# Web Push (VAPID).
# The PUBLIC half is committed in src/lib/push-public.ts on purpose: it is not
# a secret (every subscribing browser receives it) and keeping it fixed is what
# lets existing subscriptions keep working without re-subscribing.
#
# The PRIVATE half is a secret and is NOT in the repository. Set it here and in
# the deployment environment. It must be the private half of the public key in
# src/lib/push-public.ts, or the push service rejects every send with 403.
# If it is missing, Web Push reports itself unconfigured and sends nothing.
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# Optional. A mailto: or https: URL identifying the sender to the push service.
VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:support@naijamart.ng

# Shared secret that protects POST/GET /api/jobs/run (order timers + push
# flush). Generate a long random value, e.g.:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# The same value must be configured on whatever calls the endpoint (Vercel
# Cron uses its own `x-vercel-cron` marker and needs no secret).
JOBS_SECRET=

# Google Gemini API key for the AI listing assistant and the admin copilot.
# Get it from Google Cloud console → APIs & Services → Credentials.
# ROTATE THIS if the old `NEXT_PUBLIC_GEMINI_KEY` was ever committed — it was,
# and it shipped in the client bundle.
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# ── Transactional email ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NOTHING TO CONFIGURE HERE. Sign-in emails (the one-time code) are sent by
# Clerk, not by this app. There is no RESEND_API_KEY and no EMAIL_FROM in the
# code path any more.
#
# ── The custom sender name (do this in Clerk, not here) ─────────────────────
# Clerk lets you set the sender NAME on the development instance for free:
#   Clerk Dashboard → Customization → Emails (or Communication → Emails)
#   → the "Verification code" template → Sender name = "Jova"
# That is also where you write the custom message body: the template supports
# variables like {{otp_code}} and {{app.name}}, so the mail can say
#   "Your Jova sign-in code is {{otp_code}}. It expires in 10 minutes."
# This is done in the dashboard because Clerk OWNS the mail template — the app
# does not send a byte of it.
#
# ── The custom sending domain (needs a paid Clerk plan) ─────────────────────
# Sending from `no-reply@naijamart.ng` instead of Clerk's shared address
# requires a Clerk plan that includes a custom sending domain, plus DNS records
# on that domain (Clerk Dashboard → Customization → Emails → Custom domain).
# Until that is set up, codes are delivered from Clerk's own verified sender.
# That is a dashboard/billing step, not an environment variable, so there is
# nothing to add here — see README §1c.
